  "claims": [
    {
      "answer": "A service payload, not a full application. The reset handler reads VTOR, loads SP from it, calls one init function and jumps to a three-call main that ends in an endless command loop. There is no scheduler, no task table and no USB or storage code. It is a command-driven server for one client.",
      "confidence": "observed",
      "evidence": [
        "log 113 step 4",
        "this log step 1",
        "this log step 4"
      ],
      "key": "is_second_core_payload",
      "kind_basis": "listing: SC_Vector_Reset at 0x180381c0 reads 0xe000ed08, then FUN_180381a0 calls FUN_1803af90, which never returns",
      "question": "Is this a second core's payload, and what does the reset path start?"
    },
    {
      "answer": "It TRANSPORTS and CONVERTS them, and it does not deliver them to the application. Opcode 0x0f de-interleaves five groups of 16-bit values out of application RAM at pointer+0x72c, then runs a 240-iteration converter loop whose results land in an in-image array. No recovered path writes those results back.",
      "confidence": "observed",
      "evidence": [
        "this log step 4",
        "log 110 step 4"
      ],
      "key": "produces_per_key_readings",
      "kind_basis": "listing: FUN_18038b1c's five-group de-interleave and FUN_1803ae58's strobe/write/readback loop; the census resolves no write into 0x18000000..0x18037fff except one memset",
      "question": "Does it produce or transport per-key analog readings?"
    },
    {
      "answer": "No. The one demonstrated write into application RAM is a 150-byte memset to 0xff at pointer+0x3f2, on the init opcode. The travel array is at pointer+0x35c and is 150 bytes, so the memset covers the array IMMEDIATELY AFTER it and not the array itself. The key bitmap is never referenced.",
      "confidence": "observed",
      "evidence": [
        "log 110 step 4",
        "log 109",
        "this log step 3"
      ],
      "key": "writes_travel_array",
      "kind_basis": "listing: FUN_18038308 is a byte-replicating memset and is called with (pointer+0x3f2, 0x96, 0xff); aligned-word search finds the bitmap in no word of this image",
      "question": "Does anything in it write the travel array or the key bitmap?"
    },
    {
      "answer": "One block outright and two registers. 0x40040000 is touched 98 times from a single init function here and NEVER by the application or the entry image. 0x40018000 and 0x4001c000 are likewise absent from both. Everything else it touches is shared, including the 0x45000000 control block and both watchdog blocks.",
      "confidence": "observed",
      "evidence": [
        "this log step 2",
        "log 100"
      ],
      "key": "owns_what",
      "kind_basis": "census set difference against the application's and entry image's own FalchionPeripheralMap output",
      "question": "What does it own that the application does not?"
    },
    {
      "answer": "In its own RAM. The 240 conversion results are stored to an in-image array and read by one further in-image function. The only outward channels recovered are the mailbox tail index and the single memset into application RAM.",
      "confidence": "strongly-inferred",
      "evidence": [
        "this log step 4"
      ],
      "key": "data_flow_terminus",
      "kind_basis": "aligned-word search: the result array is referenced from exactly three literal-pool slots, all inside this image",
      "question": "Where does its data flow terminate?"
    },
    {
      "answer": "It settles the mechanism and not the silicon. There is now a real core-to-core-shaped channel: a ring buffer with a head owned by one image, a tail owned by another, and a start register. Whether the two run concurrently on two cores or in sequence on one is still not shown, because nothing recovered proves the application keeps executing while this image runs.",
      "confidence": "strongly-inferred",
      "evidence": [
        "log 113 step 4",
        "notes/dual-core-question.md",
        "this log step 4"
      ],
      "key": "dual_core",
      "kind_basis": "listing on both sides: the entry image's client at 0x1b7c and this image's server at 0x1803af90 implement complementary halves",
      "question": "Does this settle the dual-core question?"
    }
  ],
